I've wondered if the selection criteria for a throne room were different
than other random monster creations so I did some source diving.  It
turns out it *IS* different.  Here's the code from mkroom.c that is used
to populate a throne room with monsters:

struct permonst *
courtmon()
{
	int     i = rn2(60) + rn2(3*level_difficulty());
	if (i > 100)		return(mkclass(S_DRAGON,0));
	else if (i > 95)	return(mkclass(S_GIANT,0));
	else if (i > 85)	return(mkclass(S_TROLL,0));
	else if (i > 75)	return(mkclass(S_CENTAUR,0));
	else if (i > 60)	return(mkclass(S_ORC,0));
	else if (i > 45)	return(&mons[PM_BUGBEAR]);
	else if (i > 30)	return(&mons[PM_HOBGOBLIN]);
	else if (i > 15)	return(mkclass(S_GNOME,0));
	else			return(mkclass(S_KOBOLD,0));
}

The level_difficulty() function is the usual number that influences
random monster creation.  The ",0" part says pick a random species of
that class - There's only one species each of hobgoblin and bugbear.  I
had not previously noticed hobgoblins being other an the orc class and I
don't know if that's a special line for throne rooms only.

If the expression returns over a hundred a dragon is created.  With a
random number up to 60 that leaves 40 from the other half of the
expression.  A third of 40 is 13.

If I read the code correctly it starts being possible to get a dragon in
a throne room once the average of your XP and depth is at least 13 and
the chances group rapidly as you get deeper because of the "3*" in the
expression.

Roughly one percent of the monsters in a throne room at difficulty 13
are dragons. Since dragons are difficulty 20 that's very out of depth.
The deeper the throne room the higher the percentage of dragons in the
court.

